{"duration_api_ms":254252,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"937fae53-b3f3-4ef8-a65b-5f2809ef6571","total_cost_usd":0.29963,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12469,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":24975,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":17899},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12469,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":24975,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12469,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12469},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":24975,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":12469,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.29963,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":17899,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"observation\":\"None of the 20 evaluated designs is feasible, so there is no front yet. 10 of 20 returned no geometry. Every one of those has a cone half-angle too small for its bluntness. Fitting the 10 failures and the valid rows gives a validity rule: with nose radius = bluntness*D and shoulder radius = 0.1*D, the fore cone has positive length only if cos(theta) < 0.4/(bluntness-0.1). That means bluntness <= 0.5 is always valid, bluntness 0.7 needs theta > 48 deg, 0.9 needs > 60 deg, 1.1 needs > 66.4 deg, and 1.2 needs > 68.7 deg. The rule reproduces the sign of the fore-cone length in all 10 failures and every valid row. The constraint limits back-computed from the margins are max_g <= 12.0 and bondline T <= 450 K. Peak g depends mostly on |gamma| (about 6.3*sqrt|gamma| for D > 2.5), so gamma must be shallower than about -3.5 deg. The mass-fraction limit is not stated. Its value scales roughly as D^2 times a coefficient that falls with cone angle (about 0.089 at theta >= 62 deg, about 0.11 at theta <= 40 deg). That puts the D limit near 3.0-3.3 m. In these data, bondline T falls as D, nose radius and steepness rise. Peak flux falls with larger nose radius and larger D. Because T spans only 300-450 K against a 2.5e6 flux reference, reducing T is worth far more hypervolume, so I favour large D, large bluntness and gamma near -3.3 to -3.5, with a few shallower points to spread the front.\",\"evidence\":[\"Geometry failures C-0e7ec9742f84 (b=.94, th=55.1), C-2e989122c857 (b=.97, th=59.8), C-7fd3f4505944 (b=.75, th=36.4) and C-de1dd6ca3952 (b=.84, th=40.6) all violate cos(th) < 0.4/(b-0.1). Valid rows C-36f9165b9496 (b=.87, th=65) and C-26bc5a06b4e4 (b=.70, th=51.6) satisfy it.\",\"max_g margins imply a 12.0 limit: C-ee99f55de428 has 10.189 with margin 0.1509. Bondline margin gives 450 K: 455.54 with margin -0.01232. g vs gamma: C-f97dae207371 (-1.85 deg) 9.42, C-ee99f55de428 (-2.64) 10.19, C-63aaa0985529 (-4.10) 12.49, C-36f9165b9496 (-4.86) 13.8.\",\"Mass fraction is roughly proportional to D^2. C-63aaa0985529 (D=2.99) is at 0.97 of the limit, C-ab9827dc6929 (D=3.21) at 1.004, C-baa2f9e65f32 (D=4.01) at 1.47, C-50a0a474d0f6 (D=1.23) at 0.15. High-theta rows (C-36f9165b9496, C-12010dd082dd, C-c72c38c9187a) sit near 0.089 of the limit per m^2, against about 0.11 for C-f97dae207371 and C-63aaa0985529 at theta <= 38 deg.\",\"Peak flux fits q ~ 7e6*sqrt(sin(gamma)/(D^2*Rn)) within about 10%, where Rn = b*D. Bondline T-300 fits about 0.307*sqrt(q/|gamma_deg|). For example C-ab9827dc6929 (q=479k, gamma -4.26) gives 335*0.307 = 103 K against 103.1 K observed, and C-63aaa0985529 gives 108.8 K against 109.6 K.\",\"C-63aaa0985529 (D=2.985, gamma=-4.1) has T=409.6 K and q=515k, and misses only on max_g (-0.04). C-ab9827dc6929 misses on max_g and mass fraction. Together they show the feasible region needs D of about 3.0 or less and |gamma| of about 3.5 or less.\"],\"mechanism\":\"Peak deceleration in a ballistic entry is set mainly by entry angle. That makes max_g a ceiling on |gamma|, and the ceiling is where bondline temperature is lowest (T scales roughly as gamma^-0.25). Heat-shield mass fraction scales with the shield area, about D^2. It is lower for wide cones because they have less lateral area, which caps D near 3.0-3.3 m. Within those two limits, a large nose radius (Rn = b*D) lowers stagnation heat flux and heat load together. So the best trade-off has D near its mass limit, high bluntness with a cone angle above the validity boundary, and gamma just inside the g limit. Shallower gamma trades a little T for lower peak flux, which extends the front.\",\"uncertainty\":\"The g estimate for gamma near -3.5 (about 11.8 against a limit of 12) has only about 3% margin. I have no data on how theta and D affect g. The dependence of mass fraction on theta is inferred from correlation, not isolated. The validity rule is fitted; the length discrepancy (about 2%) suggests the shoulder radius or nose definition is slightly off, so I keep at least 1.3 deg of margin on theta. Flux and T models are rough fits (10-15% error) and small-D rows deviate. I do not know whether very high bluntness or theta near 70 deg adds flux penalties (e.g. shoulder heating) that this reduced-order model may include. Prior knowledge (Allen-Eggers scaling) supports the g and T trends, but the numeric fits come from the 10 valid rows.\",\"proposals\":[{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"Core candidate: D=3.0, b=0.9 (valid for theta>60), theta=66, gamma just inside the g limit.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.0,\"bluntness_ratio\":0.9,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":66.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.5},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":351000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":397,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Most likely first feasible point; anchors the model for g at gamma=-3.5 and mass fraction at D=3 with theta 66.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"Same geometry as the core candidate with shallower gamma=-3.2 for a safer g margin (about 11.3).\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.0,\"bluntness_ratio\":0.9,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":66.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.2},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":335000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":399,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Paired with the gamma=-3.5 point to calibrate the g, flux and T slopes in gamma.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-ab9827dc6929\",\"rationale\":\"D=3.2 with moderate bluntness 0.8 (valid for theta>55.2, using 62). Tests the upper D limit with a cone angle that reduces mass fraction.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.2,\"bluntness_ratio\":0.8,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":62.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.3},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":328000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":397,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"high\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Mass fraction is borderline (predicted about 0.93 of the limit). Shows whether higher theta really buys mass margin at D over 3.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-ab9827dc6929\",\"rationale\":\"Push D to 3.3 with b=1.0 and theta=68 (valid above 63.6) to reach the lowest T and flux.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.3,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.0,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":68.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.3},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":280000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":389,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"high\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Probes the mass-fraction boundary at large D. If feasible it is a strong front point.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"Smaller D=2.8 with b=1.0, theta=68 for a comfortable mass margin (about 0.70).\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":2.8,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.0,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":68.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.5},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":369000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":400,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Checks the D dependence of g, flux and T at high bluntness while staying safely inside the mass limit.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"D=3.1, b=1.1, theta=69.5 (valid above 66.4) for a larger nose radius at a similar D.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.1,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.1,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":69.5,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.4},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":298000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":391,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"high\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Tests whether very high bluntness with a near-maximum cone angle keeps lowering flux and T without adding a penalty.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"Maximum practical bluntness 1.2 with theta=70 (valid above 68.7, 1.3 deg margin) at D=3.1.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.1,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.2,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":70.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.4},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":286000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":389,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"high\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Explores the corner of the design space with the largest nose radius. It also tests the geometry-validity rule near its boundary.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"Moderate bluntness 0.7 with theta=56 (valid above 48.2) at D=2.9: a lower-bluntness point in the trade-off.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":2.9,\"bluntness_ratio\":0.7,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":56.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.4},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":413000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":407,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Separates the effect of bluntness and theta on flux, T and mass fraction from the high-theta designs.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-63aaa0985529\",\"rationale\":\"Low bluntness 0.6, theta=45 (valid above 36.9), D=2.9. Close to the near-feasible C-63aaa0985529 but with gamma shallowed to fix max_g.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":2.9,\"bluntness_ratio\":0.6,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":45.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.4},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":446000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":411,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Gives a low-risk feasible anchor with an independent theta and bluntness, and shows the mass-fraction coefficient at theta about 45.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-f97dae207371\",\"rationale\":\"Shallow gamma=-2.4 with D=3.0, b=1.0, theta=67 to reach the low-flux end of the front.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.0,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.0,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":67.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-2.4},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":295000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":408,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Extends front coverage toward lower flux. The high-theta choice should also fix the mass fraction that failed in C-f97dae207371.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-f97dae207371\",\"rationale\":\"Very shallow gamma=-2.0 with maximum bluntness for the lowest-flux end of the trade-off.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.0,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.2,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":70.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-2.0},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":279000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":415,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"high\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Tests how far flux can fall before T (with more heat soak) becomes the limiting objective. Shallow entry also raises mass fraction, so this probes that too.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"Smaller D=2.6 with b=1.1 and theta=68 for a large mass margin (about 0.6) and a mid-front point.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":2.6,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.1,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":68.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.3},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":382000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":404,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Maps the D dependence of g and flux at high bluntness. A smaller D may raise g.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"Theta-isolation test: identical to the core candidate except theta=62, still valid for b=0.9 (needs > 60).\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.0,\"bluntness_ratio\":0.9,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":62.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.5},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":351000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":397,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Isolates the effect of cone angle on g, flux and mass fraction (paired with the theta=66 and theta=70 points).\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"Theta-isolation test at the maximum theta=70 with everything else as in the core candidate.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.0,\"bluntness_ratio\":0.9,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":70.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.5},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":351000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":397,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Shows whether pushing theta to its bound still gives a mass-fraction benefit, or hurts flux or g.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-ab9827dc6929\",\"rationale\":\"D=3.25, b=1.0, theta=69: large-D, large-nose-radius design predicted at mass fraction about 0.94.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.25,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.0,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":69.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.5},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":295000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":389,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"high\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Tight on mass fraction and g. A feasible result would sit at the best T seen so far.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"D=2.7, b=0.85, theta=64 (valid above 57.8), gamma=-3.5. A safe mid-range design.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":2.7,\"bluntness_ratio\":0.85,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":64.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.5},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":423000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":407,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Extra feasibility insurance and a check of the D scaling for g, flux and T in the 2.6-3.0 m range.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-ab9827dc6929\",\"rationale\":\"D=3.2, b=1.15, theta=70 (valid above 67.6) at gamma=-3.4. Aims for both low T and low flux inside the mass limit.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.2,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.15,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":70.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.4},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":278000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":388,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"high\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Predicted best T and flux together. If g and mass fraction hold it should dominate most of the other candidates.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"D=3.15, b=1.05, theta=68 (valid above 65.1), gamma=-3.5 between the D=3.0 and D=3.3 probes.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.15,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.05,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":68.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.5},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":302000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":390,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Interpolates near the predicted optimum. Refines the mass-fraction and g boundaries.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"D=2.9, b=1.15, theta=70, gamma=-3.5: larger nose radius at a safer mass fraction (about 0.75).\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":2.9,\"bluntness_ratio\":1.15,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":70.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.5},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":326000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":394,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Another high-bluntness point at a smaller D, to separate nose-radius and D effects on flux and T.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0},{\"parent_id\":\"C-36f9165b9496\",\"rationale\":\"D=3.0, b=0.95, theta=65 (valid above 61.9), gamma=-3.3. A conservative variant of the core candidate.\",\"parameter_changes\":{\"diameter_m\":3.0,\"bluntness_ratio\":0.95,\"cone_half_angle_deg\":65.0,\"flight_path_angle_deg\":-3.3},\"expected_outcome\":{\"peak_heat_flux_w_m2\":332000,\"peak_bondline_temperature_k\":397,\"feasible\":true},\"uncertainty\":\"medium\",\"reason_to_simulate\":\"Fills in the bluntness and gamma grid near the predicted optimum, with a slightly larger g margin.\",\"requested_fidelity\":0}]}","ttft_ms":196342,"type":"result","duration_ms":254299,"uuid":"c3f75c11-f0d9-4cf2-adea-9c413a2bda3b","ttft_stream_ms":1374,"time_to_request_ms":45,"first_content_frame_ms":1374,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
